---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'; import { toolCatalog } from '@invokeworks/tools';
export function getStaticPaths(){ return toolCatalog.map(tool => ({ params: { name: tool.name }, props: { tool } })); }
const { tool } = Astro.props;
---
<Layout title={tool.displayName} description={tool.description}><section class="section"><div class="eyebrow">{tool.docs.category} · {tool.priceSats} sats/call</div><h1>{tool.name}</h1><p class="lead">{tool.longDescription}</p><h2>Example input</h2><pre><code>{JSON.stringify(tool.docs.examples[0], null, 2)}</code></pre>{tool.name === 'site_audit' && <><h2>Example output (shortened)</h2><pre><code>{JSON.stringify({ target: 'https://example.com/', hostname: 'example.com', dns: { records: { A: ['93.184.216.34'] } }, tls: { protocol: 'TLSv1.3', hostnameValid: true }, http: { status: 200, finalUrl: 'https://example.com/', https: true }, securityHeaders: { strictTransportSecurity: { present: false, value: null, applicable: true } }, issues: [{ severity: 'warning', code: 'missing_hsts', message: 'Consider Strict-Transport-Security where appropriate.' }], score: 95 }, null, 2)}</code></pre><p>Illustrative output. The informational score starts at 100, subtracts 25 per critical finding and 5 per warning, and stays between 0 and 100. Informational findings deduct nothing. This is not a formal security certification. Headers are recommendations, and their presence does not prove a correct policy.</p><p>TLS checks the original host; HTTP follows redirects. Unavailable sections are null and produce findings. LiveAuth meters the call at 5 sats; execution failures after authorization remain billable.</p></>}<div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/connect/">Connect</a><a class="button" href="/tools/">All tools</a></div></section></Layout>
